well let's move on now to the search for  solutions we were hearing earlier in the  show about the earthshop prize it is of  course prince william's campaign to find  solutions and innovation to tackle  climate change from across the world  around half of those finalists announced  today were from the global south well  what does that say about our search for  solutions and judy there's a real  simplicity to some of these um  shortlisted  solutions isn't there i mean costa rica  making micro payments to people to just  do their bit to protect a little bit of  rainforest  absolutely you know there are things  that only governments can do and that's  where all the protests and pressure  comes on but indeed there's some things  that individuals sections of society  like business nd  ngos and so on can do and they do it  well because they're in touch with very  local characteristics and cultural  dimensions you know take the case of 14  year old phoenicia she notices that in  her country millions of people still use  street ironing services and she's  created a solar powered ironing card in  a very sunny country to make a  difference it is amazing and you know  and i applaud prince william for saying  that he needs to be able to look his  children in the eye and say he did this  bit  you know as much as we live in a  science-dominated world it's still our  emotions and our commitment to family  that drives our strongest motivation to  act and to be fascinated inspired by  people that look like us  having amazing services it's important  to note though james isn't it that there  are no british shortlistes on that list  many of the ideas that were shortlisted  from from the global south are we a bit  arrogant in thinking that it's g7  western nations that really have the  answers here  um i think there's a risk of that i  think there's a risk of in in every part  of the world is that uh countries see  the climate crisis and the sort of the  clean tech revolution that will  hopefully help resolve it as these kind  of national local regional issues but  that's true everywhere and and one of  the most exciting things about some of  the stories we cover at business green  is the huge breadth of of innovation and  investment that we're seeing in all  parts of the world including very much  in these emerging markets and in the  developing world i mean there's one of  these talking points you get a lot from  people who are opposed to climate action  who say well what about china what about  india what about these big polluting  countries that are you know are  investing in coal yes they are but  they're also in china's case the world's  biggest renewables market the world's  biggest electric vehicle market and we  are seeing  some catalyzing progress from some of  these countries that are  in some ways because they don't have the  legacy high carbon infrastructure  they're able to leapfrog that and deploy  clean technologies at scale at pace uh  ahead of some industrialized nation so  it's much more simplistic than the idea  that the industrialized world is going  to solve this on its own and then kind  of through its largesse  help developing countries i think that  you know that is a borderline  neocolonialist way to see it necessity  is often the mother of invention isn't  it it's been really good to talk to you  both tonight judy linguang and james  murray thank you
 

